<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Differential-testing finding: xml:lang on one RDF/XML property element
  LEAKS onto the following SIBLING property element.

  XML Information Set scoping (and RDF/XML section 6.1.2, which defers to
  it) scopes xml:lang, xml:base and namespace declarations to the element
  SUBTREE. Factoidal threads them in DOCUMENT ORDER instead, so the tag
  survives past the closing tag of the element that declared it.

    bin/linux-x86_64/factoidal canonicalize tools/difftest/repros/rdfxml_xmllang_leaks_to_next_sibling.rdf

  Factoidal:   ex:plain "should have NO tag"@de      WRONG
  pyoxigraph:  ex:plain "should have NO tag"         plain literal, correct
  rdflib agrees with pyoxigraph.

  Same class as issue #325's Turtle IRIREF bug: we emit a term no other
  implementation emits, so downstream joins, canonicalization and
  entailment all operate on the wrong literal.

  Located: Parser.RDFXML.process_property_children passes
  result1.pr_state (the state RETURNED by the previous sibling) into the
  next sibling. process_property_element folds that element's own
  xml:lang / xml:base / xmlns into the state via
  update_state_from_attrs, and the mutation escapes. The scoped fields
  (lang, base_iri, namespaces, direction) must be restored from the
  enclosing state; the genuinely document-wide fields (bnode counter,
  seen rdf:ID set, has_error) must keep flowing.

  NOT caused by the #325 fix: the base binary reproduces this
  identically. It was invisible on the generated corpus only because
  those documents also tripped the #325 Name bug and were dropped whole.
  Fixing the drop made it observable.

  Note the third rdf:Description below: the tag correctly does NOT leak
  across a Description boundary, which is what pins this to the sibling
  loop rather than to a global.
-->
<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
         xmlns:ex="http://example.org/onto#">
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/s">
    <ex:tagged xml:lang="de">has a tag</ex:tagged>
    <ex:plain>should have NO tag</ex:plain>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/s2">
    <ex:plain2>also no tag</ex:plain2>
  </rdf:Description>
</rdf:RDF>
